WEBVTT

00:00:04.940 --> 00:00:12.600
I mean, imagine an 85.5 million dollar hospital, right, like complete with a gleaming FAA certified

00:00:12.600 --> 00:00:15.700
heliport in these crisp new emergency rooms.

00:00:15.700 --> 00:00:17.540
Yeah, that sounds incredible on paper.

00:00:17.540 --> 00:00:18.540
It does.

00:00:18.540 --> 00:00:24.420
But imagine that the very presence of that beautiful heliport is actually an admission

00:00:24.420 --> 00:00:26.220
of systemic failure.

00:00:26.220 --> 00:00:31.860
Today, we are looking at a situation that really defines the phrase bureaucratic catch

00:00:31.860 --> 00:00:32.860
22.

00:00:32.860 --> 00:00:33.940
It really does.

00:00:33.940 --> 00:00:38.660
So we're asking a central, frankly, provocative question for you today.

00:00:38.660 --> 00:00:43.980
Is the newly inaugurated Susana Centeno Hospital on the Puerto Rican island of Vieques a genuine

00:00:43.980 --> 00:00:46.620
turning point for healthcare equity?

00:00:46.620 --> 00:00:51.340
Or is it just a multimillion dollar photo opportunity for a lost cause?

00:00:51.340 --> 00:00:55.980
That is a question that carries, like, decades of historical trauma.

00:00:55.980 --> 00:00:59.260
And the answer depends entirely on whether you're looking at the concrete building

00:00:59.260 --> 00:01:02.660
itself or the underlying financial foundation.

00:01:02.660 --> 00:01:03.660
Right.

00:01:03.660 --> 00:01:08.340
And to find that answer, we've got a deeply divided stack of sources for you.

00:01:08.340 --> 00:01:12.420
On one side, we have these highly polished official press releases from the Puerto Rico

00:01:12.420 --> 00:01:17.980
Federal Affairs Administration, or PR FAA, along with social media posts from Governor

00:01:17.980 --> 00:01:22.780
Jennifer Gonzales-Colon and HHS Secretary Robert F. Kennedy, Jr.

00:01:22.780 --> 00:01:23.780
Right.

00:01:23.780 --> 00:01:27.380
They are definitely celebrating this as a massive historic victory.

00:01:27.380 --> 00:01:28.380
Exactly.

00:01:28.380 --> 00:01:31.220
But then on the other side, we have these really critical investigative briefing

00:01:31.220 --> 00:01:36.340
scripts from local analysts, like the Isla Nena Briefing, and a deep dive called the

00:01:36.340 --> 00:01:38.140
Healthcare Paradox.

00:01:38.140 --> 00:01:42.500
So the mission of our deep dive today is to basically cut through that ceremonial

00:01:42.500 --> 00:01:44.380
ribbon and examine the receipts.

00:01:44.380 --> 00:01:46.540
Yeah, we have to look at the math.

00:01:46.540 --> 00:01:50.420
We're looking specifically at whether federal officials will finally fix the rigged Medicare

00:01:50.420 --> 00:01:55.980
Advantage funding formula that just structurally starves Puerto Rico's medical system.

00:01:55.980 --> 00:01:58.300
And to be clear, we aren't taking sides here.

00:01:58.300 --> 00:02:02.500
We're just impartially reporting on what these conflicting sources are telling us.

00:02:02.500 --> 00:02:05.940
Which is really the invisible engine driving this entire story, right?

00:02:05.940 --> 00:02:06.940
Totally.

00:02:06.940 --> 00:02:08.860
So let's set the scene for you.

00:02:08.860 --> 00:02:11.140
It is September 2026.

00:02:11.140 --> 00:02:14.700
Over the weekend, federal and local officials gathered on the island of Yicas to open

00:02:14.700 --> 00:02:18.660
this new $85.5 million facility.

00:02:18.660 --> 00:02:23.700
And this is arriving nine years after Hurricane Maria completely destroyed the island's previous

00:02:23.700 --> 00:02:24.700
clinic.

00:02:24.700 --> 00:02:25.700
Nine long years.

00:02:25.700 --> 00:02:26.700
Okay.

00:02:26.700 --> 00:02:27.700
Let's unpack this.

00:02:27.700 --> 00:02:32.100
Because to understand why this single building carries so much weight and, you know, why you

00:02:32.100 --> 00:02:36.860
should care about how this bureaucracy operates, even if you live thousands of miles away,

00:02:36.860 --> 00:02:40.340
we really have to look at the nine years of silence that preceded it.

00:02:40.340 --> 00:02:44.300
Yeah, to understand the significance of this ribbon cutting, you can't just look at

00:02:44.300 --> 00:02:45.300
the new facility.

00:02:45.300 --> 00:02:48.180
You have to examine the ruin that it replaced.

00:02:48.180 --> 00:02:53.140
The history of health on Vieques, which is often called the Isla Nena, is remarkably

00:02:53.140 --> 00:02:54.140
grim.

00:02:54.140 --> 00:02:57.700
The original Susana Centeno Center opened way back in 1996.

00:02:57.700 --> 00:03:03.660
I was reading through the background on that 96 clinic and, I mean, calling it a hospital

00:03:03.660 --> 00:03:05.620
feels like a massive stretch.

00:03:05.620 --> 00:03:06.860
Oh, absolutely.

00:03:06.860 --> 00:03:10.700
It was a classic election year opening, meaning, you know, they cut the ribbon, but

00:03:10.700 --> 00:03:13.380
it took a full year just to actually start delivering services.

00:03:13.380 --> 00:03:14.860
Wait, really a whole year?

00:03:14.860 --> 00:03:15.860
Yeah.

00:03:15.860 --> 00:03:18.540
And even then it didn't seem equipped for complex care at all.

00:03:18.540 --> 00:03:22.620
It lacked robust dialysis capabilities, cancer care was practically non-existent

00:03:22.700 --> 00:03:25.220
and maternity care was severely limited.

00:03:25.220 --> 00:03:26.220
Wow.

00:03:26.220 --> 00:03:28.980
Broken air conditioning would routinely shut down the X-ray wards.

00:03:28.980 --> 00:03:32.500
If a resident had anything truly serious, they were getting on a boat or a plane

00:03:32.500 --> 00:03:34.300
to the main island of Puerto Rico.

00:03:34.300 --> 00:03:36.700
And then September 2017 happens.

00:03:36.700 --> 00:03:37.700
Right.

00:03:37.700 --> 00:03:39.620
Hurricane Maria finished the building off entirely.

00:03:39.620 --> 00:03:42.700
So for years after the storm, the government's solution was to park a

00:03:42.700 --> 00:03:45.420
temporary clinic inside a storm shelter.

00:03:45.420 --> 00:03:49.060
A storm shelter for an entire island's medical needs.

00:03:49.060 --> 00:03:50.060
Literally a shelter.

00:03:50.060 --> 00:03:54.060
The dialysis patients spent over a year having to cross the sea three times a week

00:03:54.060 --> 00:03:55.060
just to survive.

00:03:55.060 --> 00:03:56.780
It's exhausting just to think about.

00:03:56.780 --> 00:03:58.860
And several died making that journey.

00:03:58.860 --> 00:04:03.580
But the tragic catalyst that truly forced the government's hand happened in January

00:04:03.580 --> 00:04:04.580
2020.

00:04:04.580 --> 00:04:05.580
Right.

00:04:05.580 --> 00:04:06.580
This is where it gets really heartbreaking.

00:04:06.580 --> 00:04:11.300
A 13-year-old girl named Jidalee's Moreno Ventura started having seizures.

00:04:11.300 --> 00:04:12.540
She couldn't breathe.

00:04:12.540 --> 00:04:16.460
She was rushed to this makeshift shelter clinic, but they didn't have a working

00:04:16.460 --> 00:04:17.460
ventilator.

00:04:17.460 --> 00:04:18.780
That is absolutely devastating.

00:04:18.820 --> 00:04:20.740
A 13-year-old girl.

00:04:20.740 --> 00:04:24.100
Her family was forced to manually bag oxygen for her while they waited for an

00:04:24.100 --> 00:04:25.300
air ambulance to arrive.

00:04:25.300 --> 00:04:26.300
Oh my God.

00:04:26.300 --> 00:04:29.500
Tragically, she died before she could get the care she needed.

00:04:29.500 --> 00:04:32.500
And here is the detail that the investigative briefings highlight with

00:04:32.500 --> 00:04:33.500
heavy scrutiny.

00:04:33.500 --> 00:04:34.500
Okay.

00:04:34.500 --> 00:04:39.900
FEMA obligated $39.5 million for reconstruction just two weeks after

00:04:39.900 --> 00:04:40.900
her death.

00:04:40.900 --> 00:04:41.900
Two weeks.

00:04:41.900 --> 00:04:46.100
I mean, after nine years of waiting, it took a highly publicized tragedy

00:04:46.100 --> 00:04:48.380
for the paperwork to suddenly clear.

00:04:48.380 --> 00:04:50.740
It's hard not to be cynical about that timing.

00:04:50.740 --> 00:04:54.300
This is where I really have to push back on the celebratory tone of those

00:04:54.300 --> 00:04:56.300
official press releases.

00:04:56.300 --> 00:04:59.540
Like reading them, you'd think the government just achieved some miracle

00:04:59.540 --> 00:05:01.060
of modern administration.

00:05:01.060 --> 00:05:03.340
Yeah, they paint a very different picture.

00:05:03.340 --> 00:05:04.580
But think about it like this.

00:05:04.580 --> 00:05:08.980
For you listening, it's like having an absentee landlord who just ignores

00:05:08.980 --> 00:05:12.340
a collapsed roof in your apartment for a decade.

00:05:12.340 --> 00:05:15.620
Then when the ceiling actually falls and hurts someone, they finally bring

00:05:15.620 --> 00:05:18.660
over a tarp and demand you throw them a parade.

00:05:18.660 --> 00:05:20.740
That's a very apt analogy.

00:05:20.740 --> 00:05:24.260
The local briefings point out that the military left Vieques over two decades

00:05:24.260 --> 00:05:25.260
ago.

00:05:25.260 --> 00:05:29.460
So does evicting a military tenant inherently equal competent civilian

00:05:29.460 --> 00:05:30.860
administration?

00:05:30.860 --> 00:05:34.540
Or did the island just trade one form of neglect for another?

00:05:34.540 --> 00:05:37.780
What's fascinating here is how perfectly the specific federal

00:05:37.780 --> 00:05:41.620
official who just cut that ribbon embodies that exact systemic irony.

00:05:41.620 --> 00:05:43.340
Oh, this is the best part.

00:05:43.380 --> 00:05:46.980
We are talking about the current Secretary of Health and Human Services,

00:05:46.980 --> 00:05:48.660
Robert F. Kennedy Jr.

00:05:48.660 --> 00:05:51.820
The history here is just cinematic.

00:05:51.820 --> 00:05:55.780
Let's wind the clock back for a second to April 2001.

00:05:55.780 --> 00:05:58.620
Kennedy wasn't a cabinet secretary back then, right?

00:05:58.620 --> 00:06:01.860
He was an environmental lawyer fiercely protesting the US Navy's

00:06:01.860 --> 00:06:05.060
use of Vieques as a live impact bombing range.

00:06:05.060 --> 00:06:06.060
Right.

00:06:06.060 --> 00:06:08.940
And those protests exploded after a civilian security guard, David

00:06:08.940 --> 00:06:14.700
Sanes Rodriguez, was killed by two 500-pound dummy bombs in 1999.

00:06:14.700 --> 00:06:18.860
So in 2001, Kennedy, along with actor Edward James Olmos and

00:06:18.860 --> 00:06:22.540
union leader Dennis Rivera, they actually hired a fishing boat.

00:06:22.540 --> 00:06:23.580
Evaded the Coast Guard.

00:06:23.580 --> 00:06:26.260
Evaded the Coast Guard and trespassed right onto the

00:06:26.260 --> 00:06:29.100
restricted bombing range to physically stop the military

00:06:29.100 --> 00:06:30.020
exercises.

00:06:30.020 --> 00:06:33.500
And he paid a very real price for that trespass.

00:06:33.500 --> 00:06:36.740
Chief United States District Judge Hector Lafitte sentenced Kennedy

00:06:36.740 --> 00:06:39.900
to 30 days in the Guainabo Federal Detention Center.

00:06:39.900 --> 00:06:41.900
And his wife was pregnant at the time, right?

00:06:41.900 --> 00:06:42.420
Yes.

00:06:42.420 --> 00:06:45.900
His wife actually gave birth to their son while he was incarcerated,

00:06:45.900 --> 00:06:49.420
naming him Aidan Common Vieques Kennedy.

00:06:49.420 --> 00:06:52.020
He walked out of that jail flashing a peace sign,

00:06:52.020 --> 00:06:54.820
holding a press conference saying the Navy was leaving, quote,

00:06:54.820 --> 00:06:56.420
poison behind.

00:06:56.420 --> 00:06:59.660
Which brings us to a staggering contradiction.

00:06:59.660 --> 00:07:02.940
Kennedy went to jail arguing that the Navy was leaving poison

00:07:02.940 --> 00:07:06.180
and that public health was the ultimate moral reason

00:07:06.180 --> 00:07:07.900
to stop the bombing.

00:07:07.900 --> 00:07:10.260
The Navy eventually left in 2003.

00:07:10.260 --> 00:07:10.940
Right.

00:07:10.940 --> 00:07:13.780
Now, 25 years later, he returns to the island.

00:07:13.780 --> 00:07:16.460
But this time, he is the cabinet officer overseeing

00:07:16.460 --> 00:07:20.500
the very federal health finance systems, Medicare, Medicaid,

00:07:20.500 --> 00:07:22.940
that will determine if this new hospital in Vieques

00:07:22.940 --> 00:07:24.500
actually survives.

00:07:24.500 --> 00:07:26.620
It's an incredible full circle moment.

00:07:26.620 --> 00:07:28.180
But we have to be impartial here

00:07:28.180 --> 00:07:30.500
and look at what the local investigative briefs are

00:07:30.500 --> 00:07:32.740
saying versus the official celebration.

00:07:32.740 --> 00:07:33.380
Of course.

00:07:33.420 --> 00:07:36.300
The Trump administration and Governor Gonzales Cologne

00:07:36.300 --> 00:07:39.260
are celebrating this as a massive promise kept.

00:07:39.260 --> 00:07:41.660
But the critical briefs are saying, hold on,

00:07:41.660 --> 00:07:44.100
celebrating the Navy's departure back in 2003

00:07:44.100 --> 00:07:46.020
was actually premature.

00:07:46.020 --> 00:07:47.380
Because of the aftermath.

00:07:47.380 --> 00:07:48.340
Exactly.

00:07:48.340 --> 00:07:50.940
There is still unexploded ordinance on the island.

00:07:50.940 --> 00:07:52.820
And then there's the issue of cancer rates,

00:07:52.820 --> 00:07:55.260
which Kennedy himself alluded to with his comments

00:07:55.260 --> 00:07:56.860
about poison.

00:07:56.860 --> 00:07:58.380
I looked at the data in our sources

00:07:58.380 --> 00:08:02.060
and the numbers are incredibly complex.

00:08:02.060 --> 00:08:04.260
The cancer data is heavily debated,

00:08:04.260 --> 00:08:07.620
which is exactly why the local briefings remain so cynical.

00:08:07.620 --> 00:08:10.500
Let's walk through it because the timeline is wild.

00:08:10.500 --> 00:08:12.740
If we walk through the historical timeline provided

00:08:12.740 --> 00:08:16.300
in the source material, you see massive fluctuations.

00:08:16.300 --> 00:08:19.620
From 1960 to 1979, the incidence of cancer

00:08:19.620 --> 00:08:22.420
was actually reported to be lower in Vieques

00:08:22.420 --> 00:08:24.060
than in the rest of Puerto Rico.

00:08:24.060 --> 00:08:25.100
OK, lower.

00:08:25.100 --> 00:08:30.380
But then between 1985 and 1994, it changes, right?

00:08:30.420 --> 00:08:34.180
Yeah, rates rose above the federal ATSDR alert level.

00:08:34.180 --> 00:08:36.260
Right, prompting the legislature to order

00:08:36.260 --> 00:08:37.820
an epidemiologic study.

00:08:37.820 --> 00:08:38.700
Correct.

00:08:38.700 --> 00:08:40.860
By the early 90s, a house-to-house census

00:08:40.860 --> 00:08:43.940
found that residents were 27% more likely to have cancer

00:08:43.940 --> 00:08:45.420
than the rest of Puerto Rico.

00:08:45.420 --> 00:08:48.700
We're talking 341 cases per 10,000 residents

00:08:48.700 --> 00:08:51.780
versus 270 for the main island.

00:08:51.780 --> 00:08:53.860
That was about 40 new cases a year,

00:08:53.860 --> 00:08:55.540
according to the records.

00:08:55.540 --> 00:08:56.780
Exactly.

00:08:56.780 --> 00:09:00.180
Moving into the 1995 to 1999 window,

00:09:00.180 --> 00:09:02.940
a registry epidemiologist named Neda Figueroa

00:09:02.940 --> 00:09:06.380
separately described the incidence as 31% higher.

00:09:06.380 --> 00:09:08.140
And the other source for that same window said

00:09:08.140 --> 00:09:14.060
inference was 26% higher, with mortality 35% higher.

00:09:14.060 --> 00:09:16.620
That is a terrifying spike for a small community.

00:09:16.620 --> 00:09:17.660
It is.

00:09:17.660 --> 00:09:20.540
But the sources also highlight a very specific study window

00:09:20.540 --> 00:09:23.140
from 1992 to 1997.

00:09:23.140 --> 00:09:24.420
Right, I saw that.

00:09:24.420 --> 00:09:27.340
Why is that specific five-year window so crucial?

00:09:27.340 --> 00:09:29.780
It's the only period in a long-term series

00:09:29.780 --> 00:09:33.820
stretching from 1987 to 2011 where total cancer, lung

00:09:33.820 --> 00:09:36.700
and bronchus and colon rates were statistically higher

00:09:36.700 --> 00:09:37.700
than the main island.

00:09:37.700 --> 00:09:38.940
Statistically higher.

00:09:38.940 --> 00:09:43.020
Yeah, lung and bronchus cancer was about 2.8 times

00:09:43.020 --> 00:09:45.260
the Puerto Rico rate in women under 50

00:09:45.260 --> 00:09:48.780
and about twice the rate in men ages 50 to 64.

00:09:48.780 --> 00:09:51.620
With numbers like that, it's completely understandable

00:09:51.620 --> 00:09:53.740
why the community feels poisoned.

00:09:53.740 --> 00:09:56.180
But then as we move into the 2000s,

00:09:56.180 --> 00:09:58.820
the data seems to shift dramatically again.

00:09:58.860 --> 00:10:00.140
It does.

00:10:00.140 --> 00:10:03.860
From 2000 to 2004, the incidence was still reported

00:10:03.860 --> 00:10:06.940
as 19% higher with men 40% higher.

00:10:06.940 --> 00:10:11.380
But then fast forward to the 2008 to 2012 window.

00:10:11.380 --> 00:10:12.980
What happened then?

00:10:12.980 --> 00:10:15.820
The Puerto Rico Cancer Registry actually placed VA case

00:10:15.820 --> 00:10:18.220
in the lowest quarter of municipalities for cancer

00:10:18.220 --> 00:10:19.180
incidents.

00:10:19.180 --> 00:10:21.620
And looking at a massive 21-year stretch

00:10:21.620 --> 00:10:25.260
from 2000 to 2021, the age-adjusted incidence

00:10:25.260 --> 00:10:27.540
was actually lower in VA case.

00:10:27.540 --> 00:10:28.700
Wait, lower?

00:10:28.700 --> 00:10:29.620
Yeah.

00:10:29.620 --> 00:10:34.860
296 per 100,000 compared to 309 for the main island.

00:10:34.860 --> 00:10:36.380
Wait, I need to pause here

00:10:36.380 --> 00:10:39.820
because there's a huge spike reported in 2023.

00:10:39.820 --> 00:10:42.740
The data shows 54 cases in just one year,

00:10:42.740 --> 00:10:47.020
causing the crude rate to jump to 676 per 100,000.

00:10:47.020 --> 00:10:50.780
Right, and the age-adjusted rate was 362.4

00:10:50.780 --> 00:10:54.020
versus 327 for the main island.

00:10:54.020 --> 00:10:57.460
So how do we square a massive spike in 2023

00:10:57.500 --> 00:11:00.300
with a two-decky trend of lower incidence?

00:11:00.300 --> 00:11:03.180
This is where statistical literacy is vital.

00:11:03.180 --> 00:11:05.700
Experts note that pulling data from a single year

00:11:05.700 --> 00:11:08.780
in a tiny population of roughly 8,000 people

00:11:08.780 --> 00:11:10.460
makes the rate highly unstable.

00:11:10.460 --> 00:11:11.220
Ah, I see.

00:11:11.220 --> 00:11:14.180
One bad year can violently skew a crude rate,

00:11:14.180 --> 00:11:16.100
which doesn't account for the aging of the population

00:11:16.100 --> 00:11:18.380
in the way an age-adjusted rate does.

00:11:18.380 --> 00:11:21.140
Over the long trend from 87 to 2011,

00:11:21.140 --> 00:11:24.500
the annual change was actually negative 1% in VA case

00:11:24.500 --> 00:11:27.140
and plus 1% in Puerto Rico.

00:11:27.140 --> 00:11:30.460
Okay, so the epidemiological data fluctuates wildly

00:11:30.460 --> 00:11:33.100
based on the exact window you study,

00:11:33.100 --> 00:11:35.900
but the fear, the distrust of the federal government

00:11:35.900 --> 00:11:38.180
and the lived experience of the residents

00:11:38.180 --> 00:11:40.300
regarding their health environment,

00:11:40.300 --> 00:11:43.700
that remains a constant, heavy presence.

00:11:43.700 --> 00:11:44.660
Absolutely.

00:11:44.660 --> 00:11:47.060
Which brings us to the actual physical building.

00:11:47.060 --> 00:11:50.820
They just spent $85.5 million to construct

